# The preset policy in kgsm-base leaves the indexer disabled: it starts building a retrieval corpus,
# which costs GPU time and disk on a host that may have wanted the assistant alone. Applying that
# policy here is what makes the state explicit rather than inherited from Arch's `disable *` default.
#
# post_install only, like every other kgsm package — the decision, once made, survives an upgrade.

post_install() {
    systemctl preset kgsm-rag-indexer.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

    cat <<'MSG'

  kgsm-rag-indexer is installed and deliberately not running. Building an index is a decision:

    systemctl enable --now kgsm-rag-indexer.service

  It embeds against whichever backend /etc/kgsm-assistant/rag-indexer.env names, and it is told
  rather than left to infer — Ollama and llama-server expose different embedding routes.

  Changing that backend does NOT rebuild the index: the indexer is incremental by content hash and
  the header records the model name, which does not change when the server behind it does. Delete
  the .krag and re-run after a switch, or retrieval degrades quietly against vectors from the
  previous embedder.

MSG
}
